This historic trilateral meeting between China, the United States and Russia will hardly deliver any substantive agreements. It largely serves to satisfy the face-saving needs of its two guests, Putin and Trump.

The parties hold vastly different objectives. Both the United States and Russia have concrete demands, while China does not. With few bargaining chips to use against China, they can only manufacture leverage out of thin air — a practice China deeply abhors.

China hopes the world can calm down, recuperate economically, expand trade, and fully advance humanity’s technological progress.

By contrast:
Russia wants to resolve the Ukraine conflict;
The United States (along with Israel) seeks to deal with Iran and stabilize the Middle East;
Saudi Arabia aims to neutralize the Houthis;
India wants to settle its disputes with Pakistan;
Israel seeks to subdue Lebanon and Palestine;
Ethiopia hopes to address its tensions with Eritrea;
Rwanda wants to manage the situation in the Democratic Republic of the Congo;
Sudan aims to counter threats from Ethiopia and South Sudan;
Turkey seeks to neutralize Kurdish armed groups.

Myanmar, Yemen and Somalia are engulfed in ferocious civil wars.
Even Haiti’s capital, Port-au-Prince, is under gang control.

The Ukraine battlefield has turned into a meat grinder.
Missiles and drones crisscross the Middle East nonstop.
Africa is already embroiled in what amounts to an African World War, with very few countries remaining outside the fighting.

That is why this trilateral meeting will be largely symbolic, especially for Trump and Putin. It meets their political needs, and it will be nearly impossible to put forward viable substantive solutions — unless China, the United States and Russia jointly deploy troops to intervene, a scenario with zero probability.

China has no desire to take on this mess. It will step in only when the warring sides have truly fought themselves to exhaustion and are ready to lay down their arms. Otherwise, who can truly rein in these hot-headed combatants, shaped by decades of state-sponsored hatred?

And of course, China has yet to achieve national reunification with Taiwan.

